Nine tracker-sync PRs sat unmergeable, the oldest since August 24, and every
one of them was empty.

.gitattributes declares merge=union on .ops -- the point of an append-only
journal. That is a CLIENT-SIDE driver; the forge merges server-side and does not
read .gitattributes. Measured on .INV-juvul-*.ops: merge-base 32 lines, branch
tip 54, current dev 61 with different lines appended. Both sides appended at the
end of one file from a common base: a trivial union on any client, a textual
conflict on the server.

Nothing regressed. While syncs merged promptly, dev never diverged at the append
point. Once one PR sits unmerged and another sync appends to the same log, that
PR is permanently unmergeable, and recent tracker throughput closed the window.

The action is CLOSE, not merge. Two independent methods agree the nine carry
nothing: a per-file set difference finds 0 lines dev lacks, and merge-tree
produces a tree identical to dev's for all nine. This is the durability design
working -- do_sync gates its destructive cleanup on merge success (WI-lufal), so
a failed sync leaves ops in the working tree and the next sync carries them
forward. The data always arrived; the branches were litter.

find_superseded_sync_branches keys on "merging changes nothing", never on "the
forge says conflict". Conflating those is how a tidying step becomes data loss:
a branch with unique ops conflicts for the same reason as one without. Pinned by
a real conflicting fixture asserting conflict is not supersession, and by a
unique-op fixture with a second test proving the op is genuinely absent from dev
so the first cannot pass vacuously. Undecidable cases -- old git, missing base
ref, non-repo, blank base tree -- all return empty rather than guessing.

tracker sync --prune runs it explicitly, before preflight on purpose since a
superseded branch is one whose ops already landed. A successful auto-sync runs it
automatically.

The automatic call lives in _maybe_auto_sync, not do_sync. Wiring it to
do_sync's post-merge path broke 23 tests that script an exact sequence of _git
calls; do_sync implements a transaction and litter collection is not part of it.
The safety property never depended on do_sync's sync gate: a concurrent sync
pushes ops dev lacks, so its branch can never satisfy the predicate.

A failing prune cannot damage a sync that already merged -- asserted as all three
halves, since _maybe_auto_sync disables autonomous mode after enough consecutive
sync failures and a forge hiccup must never count as one.

